10
00:00:22,034 --> 00:00:25,386
the sailing master, the crew's navigator.

11
00:00:25,386 --> 00:00:28,772
Imagine you are a navigator of about thirty-five,

12
00:00:29,073 --> 00:00:30,875
aboard the same modest

13
00:00:30,875 --> 00:00:33,427
Caribbean sloop around seventeen seventeen.

14
00:00:34,044 --> 00:00:35,306
You are tall and thin,

15
00:00:35,306 --> 00:00:38,053
with curly black hair to your shoulders,

16
00:00:38,053 --> 00:00:40,393
a thin mustache and a long gray coat,

17
00:00:40,393 --> 00:00:44,095
and you are never far from a pair of brass dividers.

18
00:00:44,095 --> 00:00:46,705
Two years ago, pirates took you from a

19
00:00:46,705 --> 00:00:49,390
merchant ship because you could navigate.

20
00:00:49,390 --> 00:00:50,160
Since then,

21
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:52,769
this crew has voted you its master.

22
00:00:53,143 --> 00:00:53,471
You,

23
00:00:53,471 --> 00:00:56,752
this crew and this voyage are imagined.

24
00:00:56,752 --> 00:00:58,870
The problems come from trial records,

25
00:00:58,870 --> 00:01:00,378
eyewitness accounts,

26
00:01:00,378 --> 00:01:04,400
archaeology and historians' research on real pirate crews.

27
00:01:04,773 --> 00:01:06,688
And remember what the job was.

28
00:01:06,688 --> 00:01:08,822
Every course you set leads a crew

29
00:01:08,822 --> 00:01:11,073
of robbers toward somebody's ship.

30
00:01:11,575 --> 00:01:13,487
The sailors aboard those ships

31
00:01:13,487 --> 00:01:14,536
are not scenery,

32
00:01:14,536 --> 00:01:18,070
even when our story keeps the violence off screen.

33
00:01:18,070 --> 00:01:19,436
On a pirate ship,

34
00:01:19,436 --> 00:01:22,236
"master" did not mean owner.

35
00:01:22,737 --> 00:01:25,287
It meant the officer who sailed her.

36
00:01:25,287 --> 00:01:27,396
In the articles Johnson printed

37
00:01:27,396 --> 00:01:29,343
for Bartholomew Roberts's crew,

38
00:01:29,343 --> 00:01:31,581
the master received one and a half shares

39
00:01:31,581 --> 00:01:33,156
of a prize, the same as the

40
00:01:33,156 --> 00:01:35,059
boatswain and the gunner,

41
00:01:35,059 --> 00:01:38,492
and less than only the captain and the quartermaster.

42
00:01:38,492 --> 00:01:40,679
Our old friend Captain Charles

43
00:01:40,679 --> 00:01:41,858
Johnson is a pen name,

44
00:01:41,858 --> 00:01:45,035
and his General History of the Pyrates,

45
00:01:45,035 --> 00:01:48,222
from seventeen twenty-four, mixes fact with invention.

46
00:01:48,222 --> 00:01:49,990
But the share list matches what the

47
00:01:49,990 --> 00:01:54,105
economist Peter Leeson reproduces from the same articles:

48
00:01:54,105 --> 00:01:54,844
one and a half shares,

49
00:01:54,844 --> 00:01:58,337
for the man who keeps everybody from getting lost.

50
00:01:58,648 --> 00:01:59,944
The crew pays you well

51
00:01:59,944 --> 00:02:01,690
because it cannot replace you.

52
00:02:01,690 --> 00:02:03,372
Pirates who wanted a

53
00:02:03,372 --> 00:02:04,946
navigator usually took one.

54
00:02:04,946 --> 00:02:07,001
When Blackbeard's crew captured the

55
00:02:07,001 --> 00:02:10,818
French slave ship La Concorde in November seventeen seventeen,

56
00:02:11,339 --> 00:02:12,207
the Queen Anne's

57
00:02:12,207 --> 00:02:14,197
Revenge project says that among the ten

58
00:02:14,197 --> 00:02:17,059
Frenchmen forced to stay were navigators.

59
00:02:17,059 --> 00:02:18,553
The archaeologist Jeremy

60
00:02:18,553 --> 00:02:20,802
Borrelli writes that Blackbeard

61
00:02:20,802 --> 00:02:22,546
kept the French ship's pilot.

62
00:02:22,546 --> 00:02:24,367
On Roberts's flagship,

63
00:02:24,367 --> 00:02:26,402
the Royal Fortune, the master was a

64
00:02:26,402 --> 00:02:28,248
forced man named Harry Glasby.

65
00:02:28,248 --> 00:02:30,496
At the trial of Roberts's crews at

66
00:02:30,496 --> 00:02:32,821
Cape Coast Castle in seventeen twenty-two,

67
00:02:32,821 --> 00:02:36,275
witness after witness described him.

68
00:02:36,275 --> 00:02:38,396
You will meet him again in this story.

69
00:02:38,396 --> 00:02:40,222
He is the closest thing

70
00:02:40,222 --> 00:02:42,074
your job has to a patron saint.

71
00:02:42,074 --> 00:02:44,503
So you wake before dawn and go to work.

72
00:02:44,503 --> 00:02:46,818
At noon, you measure the height of

73
00:02:46,818 --> 00:02:50,352
the sun with a backstaff and work out your latitude:

74
00:02:50,352 --> 00:02:52,168
how far north you are.

75
00:02:52,168 --> 00:02:55,260
Royal Museums Greenwich

76
00:02:55,260 --> 00:02:57,471
explains why that part was manageable.

77
00:02:57,471 --> 00:02:59,349
Latitude could be found

78
00:02:59,349 --> 00:03:01,162
from the Sun or the pole star.

79
00:03:01,162 --> 00:03:03,696
Longitude, your position east or west,

80
00:03:03,696 --> 00:03:05,556
depended on knowing the time at

81
00:03:05,556 --> 00:03:07,310
a distant reference point,

82
00:03:07,310 --> 00:03:10,587
and at sea, nobody could know that reliably.

83
00:03:10,912 --> 00:03:13,510
The problem was so serious that in

84
00:03:13,510 --> 00:03:14,609
July seventeen fourteen,

85
00:03:14,919 --> 00:03:18,204
Parliament passed an act promising a public reward,

86
00:03:18,204 --> 00:03:19,854
worth thousands of pounds,

87
00:03:19,854 --> 00:03:23,201
to whoever could find longitude at sea.

88
00:03:23,201 --> 00:03:24,204
In seventeen seventeen,

89
00:03:24,204 --> 00:03:26,364
nobody had claimed it.

90
00:03:26,364 --> 00:03:28,039
That includes you.

91
00:03:28,396 --> 00:03:30,500
So you guess, carefully.

92
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:32,736
You throw a log line over the

93
00:03:32,736 --> 00:03:34,704
stern and count its knots against

94
00:03:34,704 --> 00:03:36,576
a sandglass to judge your speed.

95
00:03:36,576 --> 00:03:38,842
You read the compass for your heading.

96
00:03:39,458 --> 00:03:41,115
You write it all on a slate

97
00:03:41,115 --> 00:03:43,181
and add it up, hour after hour.

98
00:03:43,181 --> 00:03:45,940
Every small mistake adds up too.

99
00:03:46,443 --> 00:03:49,233
Tom calls this "doing sums at the sea."

100
00:03:49,719 --> 00:03:51,709
He is not entirely wrong.

101
00:03:51,709 --> 00:03:54,663
He asks you to teach him the compass.

102
00:03:54,663 --> 00:03:57,021
You show him the thirty-two points,

103
00:03:57,507 --> 00:03:58,176
north,

104
00:03:58,176 --> 00:04:04,047
north by east, north-northeast, and so on all the way round.

105
00:04:04,822 --> 00:04:06,446
By the time you reach south,

106
00:04:06,446 --> 00:04:08,090
he has forgotten north.

107
00:04:08,656 --> 00:04:10,864
He decides he prefers the bucket.

108
00:04:10,864 --> 00:04:12,228
The bucket has never

109
00:04:12,228 --> 00:04:14,196
asked him for a direction.

110
00:04:14,196 --> 00:04:16,372
At night, when the sky is clear,

111
00:04:16,372 --> 00:04:18,510
you take your quadrant to the rail and

112
00:04:18,510 --> 00:04:21,019
find the pole star low over the bow.

113
00:04:21,019 --> 00:04:22,388
It is the one fixed point

114
00:04:22,388 --> 00:04:24,163
in your whole working life.

115
00:04:24,617 --> 00:04:26,928
Everything else on this sloop moves,

116
00:04:26,928 --> 00:04:28,647
