276
00:10:13,887 --> 00:10:14,470
years ago,

277
00:10:14,470 --> 00:10:17,272
and remembers very fondly and very vaguely.

278
00:10:17,272 --> 00:10:19,263
You have no chart of it.

279
00:10:19,636 --> 00:10:21,143
You have his description,

280
00:10:21,143 --> 00:10:23,055
a latitude he swears is correct,

281
00:10:23,055 --> 00:10:25,279
and a great deal of open sea.

282
00:10:25,279 --> 00:10:27,344
You do the one thing you can trust.

283
00:10:27,344 --> 00:10:29,644
You sail until the sun at noon gives

284
00:10:29,644 --> 00:10:32,806
you his latitude, then follow that line west,

285
00:10:32,806 --> 00:10:34,819
and watch the horizon for two days.

286
00:10:34,819 --> 00:10:37,109
On the third morning, there it is.

287
00:10:37,109 --> 00:10:38,628
The crew cheers the captain

288
00:10:38,628 --> 00:10:40,385
for remembering it so well.

289
00:10:40,385 --> 00:10:41,968
The captain accepts.

290
00:10:41,968 --> 00:10:44,036
You go below and lie down.

291
00:10:44,036 --> 00:10:45,460
Real navigators did not

292
00:10:45,460 --> 00:10:47,972
always find their reef in time.

293
00:10:47,972 --> 00:10:52,044
Royal Museums Greenwich says the Queen

294
00:10:52,044 --> 00:10:54,540
Anne's Revenge and the sloop Adventure

295
00:10:54,540 --> 00:10:57,660
both ran aground entering Beaufort Inlet.

296
00:10:57,660 --> 00:10:59,529
Was that an accident?

297
00:10:59,529 --> 00:11:01,415
Johnson claims Blackbeard grounded

298
00:11:01,415 --> 00:11:02,876
his flagship on purpose,

299
00:11:02,876 --> 00:11:05,812
to break up the company and keep the best of the

300
00:11:05,812 --> 00:11:08,495
plunder for himself and a few friends.

301
00:11:08,495 --> 00:11:09,547
In twenty twenty,

302
00:11:09,547 --> 00:11:13,554
the East Carolina University archaeologist Jeremy Borrelli suggested,

303
00:11:13,554 --> 00:11:14,785
in a preliminary study,

304
00:11:14,785 --> 00:11:17,645
that the grounding may have been a deliberate

305
00:11:17,645 --> 00:11:19,652
move to get rid of a badly leaking ship.

306
00:11:19,652 --> 00:11:20,784
Either way,

307
00:11:20,784 --> 00:11:23,283
somebody had to steer her onto that sandbar.

308
00:11:23,283 --> 00:11:25,858
If it was deliberate, the navigator knew.

309
00:11:25,858 --> 00:11:28,539
If it was not, the navigator was blamed.

310
00:11:28,864 --> 00:11:30,106
And Johnson's book alone

311
00:11:30,106 --> 00:11:31,248
tells what happened to

312
00:11:31,248 --> 00:11:33,969
Blackbeard's own master, Israel Hands.

313
00:11:33,969 --> 00:11:34,636
One night,

314
00:11:34,636 --> 00:11:38,170
Johnson says, Blackbeard fired a pistol under the table and

315
00:11:38,170 --> 00:11:39,762
shot Hands in the knee,

316
00:11:39,762 --> 00:11:42,266
laming him for life, and explained that if he

317
00:11:42,266 --> 00:11:44,593
did not now and then kill one of his men,

318
00:11:44,593 --> 00:11:46,440
they would forget who he was.

319
00:11:47,037 --> 00:11:49,493
Every navigator in this story is valuable.

320
00:11:49,493 --> 00:11:51,921
That is not the same as being safe.

321
00:11:51,921 --> 00:11:52,957
Being valuable also

322
00:11:52,957 --> 00:11:54,500
meant you were watched.

323
00:11:54,809 --> 00:11:56,249
Glasby once tried to slip away

324
00:11:56,249 --> 00:11:57,828
when the crew was at an island in

325
00:11:57,828 --> 00:11:59,884
the West Indies, with two other men.

326
00:11:59,884 --> 00:12:01,731
They were caught and brought back.

327
00:12:02,201 --> 00:12:04,001
At Cape Coast, a witness said that

328
00:12:04,001 --> 00:12:05,746
Glasby and the two others had been

329
00:12:05,746 --> 00:12:07,407
sentenced to death by the pirates

330
00:12:07,407 --> 00:12:09,420
themselves for trying to escape,

331
00:12:09,420 --> 00:12:11,816
and that the other two were really shot for it.

332
00:12:11,816 --> 00:12:13,351
Glasby was spared.

333
00:12:13,351 --> 00:12:15,441
Johnson's book alone tells how.

334
00:12:15,441 --> 00:12:17,345
His version says Glasby was

335
00:12:17,345 --> 00:12:19,563
suspected because he was a reserved,

336
00:12:19,563 --> 00:12:21,119
sober man, in a crew where

337
00:12:21,119 --> 00:12:23,277
refusing to get drunk looked like a plot.

338
00:12:23,277 --> 00:12:25,140
At the pirates' own trial,

339
00:12:25,140 --> 00:12:26,076
one of the judges,

340
00:12:26,076 --> 00:12:29,393
Valentine Ashplant, stood up, took his pipe out of his mouth

341
00:12:29,393 --> 00:12:31,546
and swore that Glasby should not die.

342
00:12:32,113 --> 00:12:33,408
When the others objected,

343
00:12:33,408 --> 00:12:36,848
Johnson says, Ashplant pulled out a pair of pistols,

344
00:12:36,848 --> 00:12:38,661
and the court changed its mind.

345
00:12:39,116 --> 00:12:39,729
After that,

346
00:12:39,729 --> 00:12:43,106
according to a passenger's evidence in Johnson's version

347
00:12:43,106 --> 00:12:44,276
of the Cape Coast trial,

348
00:12:44,276 --> 00:12:46,289
the pirates never let Glasby off the ship,

349
00:12:46,289 --> 00:12:48,555
because he had once tried to escape.

350
00:12:48,555 --> 00:12:49,994
The navigator could take them

351
00:12:49,994 --> 00:12:51,940
anywhere in the world, except home.

352
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:53,698
You notice that your crew never

353
00:12:53,698 --> 00:12:56,046
sends you ashore for water either.

354
00:12:56,046 --> 00:12:57,635
On that island with the stream,

355
00:12:57,635 --> 00:12:59,962
you stand at the rail and look at the trees.

356
00:12:59,962 --> 00:13:01,248
You think about the boat,

357
00:13:01,248 --> 00:13:03,160
the beach and a night with no moon.

358
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:04,447
Then you think about the

359
00:13:04,447 --> 00:13:06,189
two men who went with Glasby.

360
00:13:06,547 --> 00:13:07,617
You stay where you are,

361
00:13:07,617 --> 00:13:09,478
and you hate how sensible that is.

362
00:13:09,932 --> 00:13:11,182
Volunteering did not

363
00:13:11,182 --> 00:13:13,088
make the job safe either.

364
00:13:13,542 --> 00:13:15,156
Only Johnson's book prints the

365
00:13:15,156 --> 00:13:17,312
articles of John Phillips's crew,

366
00:13:17,312 --> 00:13:20,004
which gave their master one share and a quarter.

367
00:13:20,522 --> 00:13:22,441
Johnson names that master,

368
00:13:22,441 --> 00:13:24,705
"or navigator", as John Nutt.

369
00:13:24,705 --> 00:13:26,334
In April seventeen twenty-four,

370
00:13:26,334 --> 00:13:28,396
when the captives rose up,

371
00:13:28,396 --> 00:13:32,343
the forced carpenter Edward Cheeseman threw Nutt overboard.

372
00:13:32,343 --> 00:13:34,143
The navigator who had chosen the

373
00:13:34,143 --> 00:13:36,641
life did not survive its last day.

374
00:13:36,641 --> 00:13:37,860
Phillips's crew had

375
00:13:37,860 --> 00:13:39,745
its own forced navigator.

376
00:13:40,103 --> 00:13:42,174
A Boston court heard in seventeen

377
00:13:42,174 --> 00:13:44,639
twenty-four that a captured man named

378
00:13:44,639 --> 00:13:46,985
Henry Giles was made to keep the ship's journal,

379
00:13:46,985 --> 00:13:48,477
because he was "an artist",

380
00:13:48,477 --> 00:13:51,215
an old word for a man with scientific skill.

381
00:13:51,215 --> 00:13:52,704
Two witnesses said he was

382
00:13:52,704 --> 00:13:54,679
always planning to escape.

383
00:13:55,037 --> 00:13:57,069
When the captives aboard rose up

384
00:13:57,069 --> 00:13:59,755
against Phillips's crew in April seventeen twenty-four,

385
00:14:00,097 --> 00:14:01,880
Giles was active in the rising.

386
00:14:01,880 --> 00:14:04,749
In May, he was acquitted with the others.

387
00:14:04,749 --> 00:14:05,639
At Cape Coast,

388
00:14:05,639 --> 00:14:09,420
two years earlier, Glasby's own trial was nearly a celebration.

389
00:14:09,420 --> 00:14:11,071
A ship's factor said Glasby

390
00:14:11,071 --> 00:14:12,784
had saved his brigantine when

391
00:14:12,784 --> 00:14:14,526
the pirates voted to burn her.

392
00:14:14,526 --> 00:14:15,756
In Johnson's version,

393
00:14:15,756 --> 00:14:19,608
a musician adds that during the fight with the warship Swallow,

394
00:14:19,608 --> 00:14:21,238
Glasby never touched the guns,

395
00:14:21,238 --> 00:14:24,135
and only trimmed the sails as Roberts commanded.

396
00:14:24,135 --> 00:14:25,858
The Swallow's lieutenant told

397
00:14:25,858 --> 00:14:28,045
the court what he had heard when

398
00:14:28,045 --> 00:14:29,738
he boarded the beaten pirate ship.

399
00:14:29,738 --> 00:14:31,480
Some of the pirates wanted to

400
00:14:31,480 --> 00:14:33,139
blow her up rather than surrender.

401
00:14:33,497 --> 00:14:35,094
Glasby had taken a lighted match

402
00:14:35,094 --> 00:14:37,892
out of the hand of a man heading for the powder,

403
00:14:37,892 --> 00:14:38,737
and had ordered the

404
00:14:38,737 --> 00:14:40,962
colours struck after Roberts was killed.

405
00:14:40,962 --> 00:14:42,485
Glasby was acquitted.

406
00:14:42,485 --> 00:14:42,969
In all,

407
00:14:42,969 --> 00:14:45,090
fifty-two of Roberts's men were hanged,

408
00:14:45,090 --> 00:14:46,995
and more than seventy were acquitted,

409
00:14:46,995 --> 00:14:49,712
most of them men who had been forced aboard.

410
00:14:49,712 --> 00:14:51,979
You think about who would speak for you.

411
00:14:51,979 --> 00:14:53,117
The merchant captain

412
00:14:53,117 --> 00:14:54,664
you left his quadrant.

413
00:14:55,070 --> 00:14:55,358
Tom,

414
00:14:55,358 --> 00:14:58,484
who would say something kind and nothing useful.

415
00:14:58,858 --> 00:14:59,826
Maybe the surgeon,

416
00:14:59,826 --> 00:15:01,806
who knows what it is to be kept.

417
00:15:02,212 --> 00:15:03,145
It is a short list,

418
00:15:03,145 --> 00:15:05,543
written in a hand you hope a court can read.

419
00:15:06,077 --> 00:15:07,701
Then the dealer brings the news

420
00:15:07,701 --> 00:15:08,869
you have heard before:

421
00:15:08,869 --> 00:15:11,099
the king's pardon of seventeen seventeen.

422
00:15:11,099 --> 00:15:15,513
Surrender to a governor by the fifth of September,

423
00:15:15,513 --> 00:15:16,060
seventeen eighteen,

424
00:15:16,060 --> 00:15:18,872
and earlier piracies would be forgiven.

425
00:15:18,872 --> 00:15:20,708
You read the reward list.

426
00:15:21,050 --> 00:15:22,934
As the London Gazette printed it,

427
00:15:22,934 --> 00:15:25,297
a pirate commander was worth one hundred pounds,

428
00:15:25,297 --> 00:15:27,622
and every lieutenant,

429
00:15:27,622 --> 00:15:32,085
master, boatswain, carpenter and gunner forty.

430
00:15:32,085 --> 00:15:35,843
You are on it, beside the carpenter.

431
00:15:36,457 --> 00:15:37,085
Forty pounds,

432
00:15:37,085 --> 00:15:40,012
for the only man who knows where the ship is.

433
00:15:40,610 --> 00:15:42,335
You have been undervalued before,

434
00:15:42,335 --> 00:15:43,700
but never by a king.

435
00:15:44,233 --> 00:15:45,755
The crew votes on the pardon.

436
00:15:45,755 --> 00:15:48,317
You were forced, but you were also elected master,

437
00:15:48,317 --> 00:15:49,312
and you have a vote.

438
00:15:49,312 --> 00:15:51,369
You raise your hand for the pardon.

439
00:15:51,369 --> 00:15:52,694
The pardon wins, narrowly.

440
00:15:52,694 --> 00:15:54,020
Then the captain turns to

441
00:15:54,020 --> 00:15:55,635
you and asks the question you

442
00:15:55,635 --> 00:15:57,038
have waited two years to hear.

443
00:15:57,038 --> 00:15:58,744
Which way to the governor?

444
00:15:58,744 --> 00:15:59,829
You set the course.

445
00:15:59,829 --> 00:16:00,915
You check it twice.

446
00:16:00,915 --> 00:16:03,203
It is the last course you will ever

447
00:16:03,203 --> 00:16:04,010
set for this crew,

448
00:16:04,010 --> 00:16:06,713
and for once, everyone wants to arrive at the same place.

449
00:16:06,713 --> 00:16:08,059
So why was being a pirate

450
00:16:08,059 --> 00:16:09,815
navigator worse than you think?

451
00:16:09,815 --> 00:16:11,448
Because the crew needed you too

452
00:16:11,448 --> 00:16:13,166
much to ever let you go ashore.

453
00:16:13,166 --> 00:16:14,693
Because you did the hardest

454
00:16:14,693 --> 00:16:17,538
arithmetic at sea, and they voted on the answer.

455
00:16:17,538 --> 00:16:20,204
Because you could guide them anywhere,

456
00:16:20,204 --> 00:16:21,387
except away from them.

457
00:16:21,387 --> 00:16:22,877
And because every course you

458
00:16:22,877 --> 00:16:24,946
set led to somebody else's ship.

459
00:16:24,946 --> 00:16:26,827
A good navigator could get a crew home.

460
00:16:26,827 --> 00:16:28,030
A court would ask where

461
00:16:28,030 --> 00:16:29,223
he had taken them first.

462
00:16:29,223 --> 00:16:31,779
If you want another life behind the legends,

463
00:16:31,779 --> 00:16:32,704
and another job that

464
00:16:32,704 --> 00:16:34,679
sounds better than it was, subscribe.

465
00:16:35,008 --> 00:16:36,222
This is Pirate Pages.

466
00:16:36,222 --> 00:16:38,278
The lives behind the legends.
